1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,667
The brush uncovers another copper plate, with a seal still attached.

2
00:00:09,967 --> 00:00:16,104
We are in the seventh century now, and for the king this episode is about we have something rare in ancient Indian history:

3
00:00:16,104 --> 00:00:20,165
two long written accounts, by two very different people, of the same reign.

4
00:00:26,167 --> 00:00:30,995
A court biography, the Harshacharita, written by Banabhatta, who worked for Harsha.

5
00:00:30,995 --> 00:00:37,606
A travel account by Hiuen Tsang, a Chinese Buddhist pilgrim who spent years in India and stayed at Harsha's court.

6
00:00:37,606 --> 00:00:39,216
And inscriptions and seals.

7
00:00:39,216 --> 00:00:44,849
Two of those three are by people with reasons to be generous, and the third is a foreign visitor.

8
00:00:44,849 --> 00:00:48,196
Reading them against each other is the whole method.

9
00:00:54,733 --> 00:00:59,491
Harshavardhana came to the throne about 606 CE at Thanesar, after his brother was killed,

10
00:00:59,491 --> 00:01:07,050
and soon moved his capital to Kannauj — which from this point becomes the prize that northern dynasties fight over for the next two centuries,

11
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:09,676
as the last episode of this unit will show.

12
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:19,745
He spent his early reign campaigning, and brought much of the north under his authority.

13
00:01:23,633 --> 00:01:24,826
And then he stopped.

14
00:01:24,826 --> 00:01:28,803
Moving south, he was checked at the Narmada by Pulakeshin the Second,

15
00:01:28,803 --> 00:01:35,450
the Chalukya ruler of Badami — an event recorded not by Harsha's own writers but in Pulakeshin's Aihole inscription,

16
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:38,136
which is exactly why historians trust it.

17
00:01:42,333 --> 00:01:43,975
The Narmada stayed the limit.

18
00:01:43,975 --> 00:01:50,840
It is a useful fact to carry: the north and the Deccan were separate political worlds, and the river is where they met.

19
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,700
Hiuen Tsang describes an assembly at Kannauj and a great distribution at Prayag,

20
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:03,630
where Harsha is said to have given away the contents of his treasury.

21
00:02:07,367 --> 00:02:13,411
Harsha patronised Buddhism and also supported other traditions, and he endowed the monastery at Nalanda,

22
00:02:13,411 --> 00:02:17,383
which Hiuen Tsang describes in detail as a great centre of learning.

23
00:02:17,383 --> 00:02:23,554
He was also a writer: three Sanskrit plays, including Nagananda and Ratnavali, are attributed to him.

24
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:33,202
His administration looks less like the Mauryas and more like what came after.

25
00:02:33,202 --> 00:02:37,198
Officials were increasingly paid with grants of land rather than salary,

26
00:02:37,198 --> 00:02:42,259
and subordinate rulers — feudatories — held their territories while acknowledging him.

27
00:02:44,667 --> 00:02:49,535
That is why his empire dissolves so quickly when he dies without an heir,

28
00:02:49,535 --> 00:02:56,874
around 647 CE. An empire held together by personal authority over feudatories does not survive the person.

29
00:03:01,033 --> 00:03:02,593
How big was it really?

30
00:03:02,593 --> 00:03:09,401
The older view, following Bana and Hiuen Tsang, describes a large empire across northern India.

31
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:20,646
A more sceptical reading notes that one of those sources was his employee and the other his guest, that the inscriptional evidence is thinner than the literary,

32
00:03:20,646 --> 00:03:25,821
and that what he held was overlordship over subordinate rulers rather than directly governed territory.

33
00:03:25,821 --> 00:03:29,904
The check at the Narmada is a fact; the extent north of it is argued about.

34
00:03:34,933 --> 00:03:37,751
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:37,751 --> 00:03:42,081
Two writers, one king — Bana at court, Hiuen Tsang travelling.

36
00:03:42,081 --> 00:03:44,443
Read them against each other.

37
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:50,666
Dig Quiz.

38
00:03:50,666 --> 00:03:52,122
Pause and name it.

39
00:03:52,122 --> 00:03:56,797
First: the court biography of Harsha, written by Banabhatta.

40
00:03:56,797 --> 00:03:58,553
The Harshacharita.

41
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:06,524
Second: the river at which Pulakeshin the Second checked Harsha's advance south.

42
00:04:06,524 --> 00:04:07,331
The Narmada.

43
00:04:07,331 --> 00:04:12,779
Third: the great centre of learning that Harsha endowed and Hiuen Tsang described.

44
00:04:16,167 --> 00:04:16,770
Nalanda.

45
00:04:16,770 --> 00:04:22,428
In Mains, a good question asks what Hiuen Tsang's account is worth as a source.

46
00:04:26,567 --> 00:04:33,447
Say what an outsider sees that an insider does not, and what a devout pilgrim at a friendly court is likely to emphasise.

47
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:40,602
A sealed copper plate takes its place in the gallery.

48
00:04:40,602 --> 00:04:42,715
Next gallery: south of the Narmada,

49
00:04:42,715 --> 00:04:49,469
the dynasty that ruled while some of the finest painting in the ancient world was being made in a cliff face.

50
00:04:52,267 --> 00:04:55,961
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

